# tlo: wiosna_okruszki
# rozdzial: Chapter Fifteen
# tytul: Kupala Night
{ droga_do_boru:
    All winter Urbaniak’s road lay under the snow like a scar—broad, straight, broken off abruptly at the first great pines. No one drove along it. The woodcutters from Bukowiec, who came in February for the rest of the trunks, turned back halfway down the cutting, because the horses reared and would go no further.
- else:
    The winter was long and lean. From Candlemas onwards Hanka measured out the groats by the spoonful, and in Lent they lived mostly on bran soup and sauerkraut. No one went into the young wood, which had crept up to the village on the night of the fire, not even for kindling.
}
In August Marysia would turn three, and there was not a corner of the cottage she had not poked her nose into. She spoke in whole sentences, gravely and thoughtfully, like a little housewife, and when she did not know a word she made one up herself. Hens she called “featherbeds”, and frogs “hoppers”.{ ekwipunek ? szczenie_psa: The russet dog let her drag him by the ears, the tail and the paws, and fell asleep wherever she did—under the table, in the passage, in the laundry basket.}
At Easter Jaromir carved her first shoes out of limewood—little clogs, lined inside with felt from his father’s old hat. Hanka painted them red, because everything evil is afraid of red. Marysia loved them from the very first moment. She wore them only on feast days, but she slept with them under her pillow, and in the morning, before she had even opened her eyes, she would feel with her hand to make sure they were still there.
She also had a new habit. After breakfast she would take a handful of crumbs from the bowl, go round behind the barn and scatter them under the rye. # pokaz: okruszki
“For the lady,” she would say, whenever anyone asked.
{noc_ognia == zbudzil_wies:Then Jaromir would clench his hands until the scars from the red-hot latch turned white.|Jaromir pretended not to hear.} Hanka stopped asking which lady. It was enough for her that the little one always came back—with an empty fist and dew on her bare feet. # dalej
-> wigilia_swietego_jana

= wigilia_swietego_jana
# tlo: sobotka
On the twenty-third of June the whole village smelled of herbs and smoke from early morning. The women tucked bunches of St John’s wort and mugwort behind the windows and over the doors, so that on the shortest night of the year nothing evil would cross the threshold. The boys hauled brushwood to the hillock beyond the village, right by the fields, for that was where the Midsummer bonfire was lit every year: when the fire roared up, they snatched burning brands from it and ran with them round the field balks, smoking the corn so that smut would not eat it and no evil power would nest in the ears.
Hanka plaited a red ribbon into Marysia’s hair and put the clogs on her feet—for the first time on a day that was not a feast.{ skarb_dla_corki: Round her neck, on a leather thong, {skarb_jaromira == muszelka:swung a white piece of shell|{skarb_jaromira == pioro:swung a jay’s feather|swung an old nail}} from the den in the old elder.} The little one strutted about the room like a stork, lifting her knees high, and clattered so loudly that she could be heard right out in the yard.
“Today everything’s a holiday,” said Hanka, when Jaromir raised his eyebrows. “Even you might smile for once. Let her have it.”
At dusk the fire flared up on the hillock, and a moment later red points of brands began to race along the balks, as though someone were tracing the fields in embers. # pokaz: ogien
{ uklad_z_mamunami == dar_swietojanski:
    Jaromir sat on the threshold with {sposob_na_odmienca == chrzest_odmienca:two loaves|a loaf} of bread and a wreath of rue on his knees. Every year on this night he went to the far shore of the lake, to the flat stones, and every year he came back without looking behind him, with a chill between his shoulder blades that did not leave him till morning.
}
# pytanie: What will Jaromir do?
*   [Go to the bonfire with the whole family.]
    ~ noc_kupaly = na_sobotce
    They sat down on the edge of the hillock among the neighbours, the rye field at their feet. Antek held his Franek on his knees, and the boy tugged at everyone’s beard, while Marysia rode on Jaromir’s shoulders and squealed each time one of the lads leapt over the flames. There was a smell of pitch, roasting potatoes and sun-warmed grass. Hanka laughed at something Jadwisia had said and for the first time in months looked carefree. # pokaz: rodzina
    Towards midnight Marysia slid to the ground to watch the boys with their brands. She clung tightly to her mother’s skirt—through the linen Hanka could feel her small hand, warm and sticky with honey. At some point the grip slackened. Hanka reached down to straighten the little one’s ribbon and her hand met empty air. Marysia was not beside her. All at once the fire gave no more warmth. # pokaz: pusto # dalej
*   [Stay in the cottage and bolt the door.]
    ~ noc_kupaly = w_chalupie
    # tlo: izba_kupala
    “We’ll stay at home,” said Jaromir. “We can do without the bonfire.”
    Hanka did not argue. They bolted the door and shut the shutters, and Marysia, cross with the whole world, fell asleep on the bench in her clogs, because she would not let anyone take them off. Through the cracks in the shutters squeezed the red glow of the brands running along the balks. Jaromir sat at the table with his{ siekiera_dziadka: grandfather’s} axe propped against his thigh and listened to the distant singing until his eyes began to close of their own accord.
    He was woken by cold rising from the floor. The bolt was drawn back, the door stood ajar by the width of a hand, and the bunch of mugwort Hanka had tucked above the threshold lay trampled in the passage. The bench was empty.{ ekwipunek ? szczenie_psa: The dog, who growled at every stranger, lay under the table with his head on his paws, trembling—he had not made a single sound.} # pokaz: drzwi # dalej
