    “I’ll be back directly,” said Jaromir, and kissed Marysia on the top of her head. She smelled of smoke and milk.
    # tlo: jezioro_mamun
    On the far shore of the lake it was dark and cold, as though the fires over the fields did not reach so far. He laid the bread and the wreath on the largest stone. Water lapped against the stones—once, softly, as when linen is rinsed—and a dry whisper came from the reeds. # pokaz: dar
    “This is not where you should be tonight, goodman,” said the eldest mamuna, without stepping out of the darkness. “But you have kept your word. And we shall keep ours.”
    He ran the whole way back, the blood drumming in his ears. The cottage door stood wide open. Hanka stood at the edge of the rye, her fists dug into her apron. Afterwards she swore that she had not taken her eyes off the little one for longer than a single blink—that she had only turned to the stove, because a spark had leapt out of the embers. # dalej
