Then they heard the singing.
It was Hanka singing. At least, that was how it sounded—her voice, warm and a little husky, as when she rocked Marysia to sleep. It carried from over the rye, soft and clear, humming the same tune as the little one, broken off halfway. {noc_kupaly == na_sobotce:Hanka stood beside Jaromir by the bonfire, grey as ash, and clutched at her throat.|{noc_kupaly == w_chalupie:Hanka was sitting up on the bed, suddenly wide awake, holding her throat. They ran out in front of the cottage.|Hanka was holding her throat.}} The voice was hers, every rasp, every falter on the high note—and yet she was silent. # pokaz: spiew
“Mama’s singing,” Hanka whispered, not knowing herself to whom. That was what Marysia had said last year, in the rye behind the barn, pointing her little hand away from the cottage.
The rye already stood tall, chest-high to a grown man, silver in the moonlight and heavy with dew. And in it, far off, something white—a little shift, a red ribbon—was floating between the ears towards the forest, evenly and calmly, no higher than a child and no faster. # pokaz: koszulka
