    The dog tore forward and plunged into the corn with a whine such as Jaromir had never heard from him. A moment later he came back. In his teeth he carried a small clog, wet and cold. # tobolek
- else:
    Hanka was the first to throw herself into the corn. After a dozen or so steps she stumbled over something hard and picked it up off the ground. A small clog, wet and cold. # tobolek
}
The ears before them parted, and they saw her.
~ zapiski += zytnia_baba_1
She barely rose above the rye—short, hunched, broad in the hips like a sheaf, with a face wrinkled like the baked crust of a loaf and hair of corn ears that rustled at every turn of her head. She smelled of warm grain and of earth after the harvest. In her outstretched hand she held the other clog and rocked it slowly, the way one shakes a rattle before a baby. For a single heartbeat Jaromir saw Lady Midday before his eyes—but she had been tall and white, and had looked at him like a judge. This one was smiling. Like a market wife come to collect what was owed her. # pokaz: baba
# pytanie: What will Jaromir do?
*   [Throw himself at her.]
    ~ pani_w_zbozu_noc = gonil_babe
    {noc_kupaly == w_chalupie:Jaromir sprang forward with the axe. The blade passed|Jaromir hurled himself at her bare-handed. His hands passed} through her as through a bundle of straw—there was a rustle, a scatter of grain—and she was already a few paces off, crouching among the ears and cawing with laughter like a rook on a stubble field. # pokaz: gonitwa
    He chased her until dawn. The rye lashed his face, the sharp stalks cut his hands till they bled, and the field he had known since childhood had no end: every time he drew close, the same balk rose before him, and the same field stone with a little cross gouged into it. When the rising sun struck him in the eyes, he was standing at the edge of the rye, in the very spot he had started from, alone and hoarse from shouting. On the thistles by the balk hung a red ribbon. # pokaz: swit
    ~ ekwipunek += wstazka_marysi
    His hands burned for a week after, and tiny awns from the ears festered in the cuts and could not be drawn out—they burrowed all the deeper the harder he picked at them. # dalej
*   [Follow the singing.]
    ~ pani_w_zbozu_noc = za_spiewem
    Jaromir no longer looked at her. He followed the voice—through the rye, across the fallow, through a ditch full of nettles—and the singing came neither nearer nor farther, always just beyond the next row of ears. Hanka walked behind him, holding on to his belt.
    ~ ekwipunek += wstazka_marysi
    At the edge of the young wood the singing broke off—at the very place where Marysia’s tune always broke off. On a juniper branch, at the height of a child’s hand, hung a red ribbon.{ not poszedl_po_wstazke: It was tied in a bow—just as carefully as that ribbon of Hanka’s which someone had once left for him on the fence, years ago.} # pokaz: mlodnik
    Jaromir started towards the trees. Hanka yanked at his belt so hard that he staggered.
    “Not in the dark,” she rasped. “Not in the dark, do you hear? I won’t give them the both of you.”
    Only then did he hear what her voice sounded like. It was hoarse and cracked, as if someone had torn it out of her and given back only half. Never again did Hanka sing a single lullaby. # dalej
