# tlo: rano_kupala
Over the hillock the burnt-out bonfires smouldered, the sour reek of wet ash hung in the air, and the mugwort behind the windows wilted, as though it had never had anything to guard.
Until noon everyone searched—in the meadows, in the lake, by the wells, in every barn and every ditch. {noc_ognia == ratowal_kapliczke:Szymon, his hands wrapped in linen, had Józek lead him along the stream and called out together with him.|Szymon looked under every little bridge over the stream.} Józek combed through the reeds clump by clump. {trzy_noce == czuwanie_przy_kolysce:Jadwisia|Mrs Wójcik} did not leave Hanka’s side for a moment and kept squeezing her hands, for Hanka was shaking all over, as if in a fever, though the sun had been scorching since morning. # pokaz: szukaja
No one set foot in the young wood.
~ zapiski += zytnia_baba_2
“The rye mother didn’t take her for herself,” old Staś said at last. He was sitting on a stump by the barn, turning Marysia’s clog over in his fingers. “Mostly she catches children in the corn and leaves them there. This one she didn’t leave. This one she led across.” He raised his eyes to Jaromir. “She was carrying her to someone.” He was silent a long while.{ stas_zaufanie: “When I was a lad, your grandfather never fobbed me off with a tale when I asked what walks the marshes. So I’ll not fob you off neither.”} “Somebody carried me once, too. I was four, maybe. I came back after three nights, smelling of resin, and to this day I don’t know where from. Mother made me swear I’d tell nobody. And I wasn’t the only one it happened to, only that’s not something folk in the village talk about.” # pokaz: stas
{ dar_pominiety:
    Jaromir went cold. Last night he had not taken the bread to the mamunas—for the first time since he had promised. “If you forget even once, we’ll come for what’s ours.” He no longer knew whether it was the forest that had taken Marysia—or the lake.
}
Hanka came out in front of the cottage. Her eyes were dry and her hair tightly plaited, as if for work. She stopped before Jaromir.
“You’re going after her,” she said firmly. “But you won’t go blind. First you’ll find out what to be afraid of.”
# pytanie: Whom will Jaromir ask for advice?
*   [Ride to Bukowiec, to Jagna.]
    ~ rada_przed_borem = rada_jagny
    ~ dlug_u_jagny = true
    # tlo: targ_jagna
    Jagna had come to the Midsummer fair with her herbs again and was sitting by the church, behind baskets full of St John’s wort. When she saw Jaromir, she set a basket aside and studied him with her good eye. {zaufal_jagnie:“I knew you’d come,” she said quietly.|“Now you come,” she said, without anger. “And a year ago you wouldn’t listen to me.”}{ los_figurki == figurka_u_jagny: “That doll lies far from here, in the ground under the church threshold,” she added. “This isn’t its doing.”} She heard him out without a word. # pokaz: jagna
    She drew closer.
    “The forest takes children when its lord wakes,” she whispered. “It doesn’t kill them. It keeps them. Go in there, and the paths will pretend to lead you on, but they’ll turn you back. Whatever you find in the forest, don’t eat it and don’t drink it, even if you’re dropping with hunger.” She hesitated. “And when you come back, you’ll bring me the first thing you carry out of the forest. Whatever it may be. That’s the price.”
    Jaromir agreed. Only on the way home did it occur to him that there was just one thing he cared about carrying out of the forest. # dalej
