# tlo: torba_noc
~ zapiski += pan_lasu_7
That night Hanka sewed Jaromir a linen bag. The needle kept slipping from her fingers; once she drove it into her thumb and did not even notice—a rusty dot was left on the cloth. Into the bag she put bread, cheese, salt tied up in a rag and a bunch of fresh mugwort. On top she laid Marysia’s warm woollen frock, folded carefully, as if to lay it away in the chest. # pokaz: welniak
“So she won’t be cold,” she said. “When you find her.”
{ ekwipunek ? ksiega:
    That day, while it was still light, Jaromir read Mr Ignacy’s book by the open window—about Borowy, about paths that turn back, about people who came home from the forest after many years and did not know their own children. When dusk began to fall, he closed it and set it aside to take on the road.
}
On the table stood a single clog. Jaromir could not tear his eyes from it. All he could think was that Marysia was barefoot, and that the dew in the forest is cold even in June.{ skarb_dla_corki: And that round her neck she wore his {skarb_jaromira == muszelka:shell|{skarb_jaromira == pioro:feather|nail}}—that in the forest, among strangers, she had at least that much of home with her.}
{ ekwipunek ? zoladz: From behind the beam over the stove he took the acorn from beneath the Old Oak, kept since his boyhood—still warm—and hid it inside his shirt.}
Before dawn he rose, took down from its peg the axe{ siekiera_dziadka: that had been his grandfather’s}{ ekwipunek ? krzesiwo:, slipped the flint and steel into his pocket} and went out in front of the cottage. Hanka stood in the doorway.{ ekwipunek ? szczenie_psa: The dog sat at her feet, trembling, but would not leave the door.} # pokaz: swit
“The pine splint will burn every night,” she said. “Until you both come back.”
Jaromir nodded and set off across the dew-wet meadow, through the rye, towards the young wood. # dalej
